smbpal (0.2.6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * macOS groundwork. On Linux this release changes nothing you can see,
    except that the package now ships an `smbpal-agent` command which exists
    for macOS and says so when it is run here.
  * Mounting on macOS is implemented, and it runs in a per-user agent rather
    than in the daemon. Two measurements put it there: mounting on macOS
    needs no elevation at all, and the login Keychain -- where the password
    belongs -- cannot be read by root.
  * The agent installs itself with `smbpal-agent --install`, which writes a
    LaunchAgent and loads it. `--status` says whether launchd has it, is
    retrying it after a failure, or is pointing at a program that has moved,
    because `launchctl` reports all three the same way.
  * `smbpal` no longer tells a Mac user to run `systemctl`. There is no
    SMBPal daemon on macOS yet, and naming a command the platform does not
    have sent people looking for something they could not install.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:50:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Three messages say what they mean. Each was accurate and each named a
    symptom that several different causes share, so it read as though one
    cause had been identified and sent people to the wrong fix.
  * A refused connection now names both of the things that cause one. A
    server refusing a connection gives no reason, so SMBPal could not tell
    a stopped share from a blocked port -- and the two are fixed in
    opposite ways. It now says so, and points at `smbpal status` on the
    other machine, which can tell.
  * Disconnect says what it did. Disconnecting unmounts the share and
    leaves it ready to mount again the moment anything opens the folder,
    which on some desktops happens immediately -- so the button appeared to
    do nothing at all. The window now says what happened.
  * `smbpal browse` no longer says a machine is not running SMBPal when it
    is. The column reported whether a machine was sharing something at that
    moment, under a heading that promised something else, and answered "no"
    for the computer you typed the command on. It is now headed SHARING
    NOW.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0100

smbpal (0.2.4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The window says why there is no tray icon. On GNOME nothing shows tray
    icons without an extension, so SMBPal simply had none and said nothing
    about it. The window now explains that once, with the command for your
    system and the log out that has to come between installing the
    extension and enabling it, and it can be dismissed for good. Nothing is
    missing without a tray icon: the window is the whole of SMBPal.
  * A share you add is reported as served only if something is serving it.
    On Fedora and its family a package never starts a service, so Samba
    arrives installed and stopped: a share was configured correctly,
    reported as served, and reachable by nobody. SMBPal now says when Samba
    is not running, and gives the command that starts it.
  * A folder shared from your home directory on a system with SELinux is
    flagged before you find out the hard way. The share mounts and
    authenticates and then refuses every write, which looks like a broken
    server rather than a security policy. SMBPal now says what the policy
    says and gives the two commands that fix it. It does not change the
    policy itself.
  * A connection you remove and add again appears in the file manager
    again. It was being remembered as already done, so it stayed configured
    but invisible until the daemon was restarted.
  * A connection whose mount is refused no longer looks like one you
    disconnected. SMBPal recorded a mount as made as soon as it asked for
    it, so a mount the server refused was indistinguishable from one that
    worked, and it was never tried again. It now waits to see the mount,
    reports a refusal with the reason, and tries again as soon as you store
    the credentials that would fix it.
  * "membership of the 'smbpal' group is required" now says which of three
    things is wrong: SMBPal is not fully installed, you are not in the
    group, or you are but this session began before you were added.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 23:30:35 +0100

smbpal (0.2.3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Connecting to a share with the wrong password no longer breaks the
    connection. Correcting the password reported "the daemon hit an
    internal error" and changed nothing: a mount that has just failed
    leaves the folder it mounts on in a state the daemon could not read,
    and it stopped there rather than writing the corrected credentials.
    Found on a Raspberry Pi running Bookworm.
  * A connection whose password is refused is no longer added twice. The
    Connect form said only that it had failed, which read as though nothing
    had happened, so filling it in again made a second connection to the
    same share. It now says the connection was made and is waiting for a
    password, and trying again corrects that connection instead of adding
    another.
  * SMBPal refuses a second connection to a share it is already connected
    to, and says which one already has it.
  * A change the daemon could not apply is no longer left in the
    configuration. Failures it expected were already undone; failures it
    did not expect were not, which is the case where least is known about
    what reached the machine.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sun, 20 Sep 2026 14:38:03 +0000

smbpal (0.2.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A folder shared from the window can now be opened from other devices.
    Samba keeps its own passwords, separate from the login password, and
    the Share a folder form never asked for one, so on a fresh machine
    nobody could sign in to a share made there. The form now fills in your
    account, and if that account has no SMB password it asks for one and
    sets it before the share is added.
  * A share that nobody can sign in to is flagged in the window with a Set
    password button, and in "smbpal status" with the command that fixes it.
    A share that works says which account to sign in as, and that it takes
    the SMB password rather than the login password.
  * The window's header shows how to reach this computer from another
    device: its .local name, as Avahi actually answers to it, and its
    addresses. "smbpal status" prints the same.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 20:21:03 +0000

smbpal (0.2.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The window and the tray no longer report the daemon as lost after two
    minutes in which nothing changed. The connection they listen on for
    updates was timing out after 130 quiet seconds, so "no reply from the
    daemon within 130s" flashed up in the window, and the tray briefly said
    the service was not running, on a machine where nothing was wrong.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 17:55:28 +0000

smbpal (0.2.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The tray icon has a menu: New Share, New Connection, Manage and Close.
    New Share and New Connection open the window with that form already up,
    or bring the form up in the window if it is open. Manage opens the window
    and is greyed out while it is already open. A left click still opens the
    window; on Ubuntu's GNOME, whose tray extension sends a single click to
    the menu, a double click does.
  * Connections now appear in the file manager without being opened first.
    A connection is mounted once, the first time the daemon sees it ready,
    without holding up boot: "When this network is available" waits until
    the server answers, "Always" does not wait, and "Only when I ask" is
    never mounted by itself. Ejecting it from the file manager is respected
    until it is next opened.
  * The window keeps its scroll position when a connection changes state,
    instead of jumping back to the top on every update.
  * Each release now carries a SHA256SUMS file and a build attestation, so a
    download can be checked for damage and traced to the workflow run that
    built it.
  * Every build is installed, used to share a folder, removed and purged on
    Debian 12, Debian 13, Ubuntu 24.04 and Ubuntu 26.04, with smb.conf
    compared byte for byte before and after. A release is not published
    unless all four pass. That covers installation and removal; the desktop
    itself is tested by hand on Raspberry Pi OS Trixie on a Raspberry Pi 4.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sat, 19 Sep 2026 17:02:53 +0000

smbpal (0.1.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * First release. SMBPal shares folders over SMB and connects to shares on
    other machines, from a window, a tray icon or a command line.
  * The daemon, the command line, the GTK4 window and the tray icon are one
    package and one version. They speak to each other over a socket, and
    shipping them separately is how a client and a daemon come to disagree.
  * Serving a folder writes to /etc/samba/smbpal.conf, which this package owns
    completely. The distribution's smb.conf gains a single "include" line and
    is otherwise never read, rewritten or reformatted. Shares written by hand
    are shown, marked as not managed by SMBPal, and left alone; a name that
    would shadow one is refused.
  * Connecting to a remote share writes a systemd .mount and .automount unit
    rather than an fstab line, so a share is attached when something looks at
    it and its failures are visible to journalctl. A mount SMBPal did not make
    is never unmounted, and an unreachable server is reported as unreachable
    rather than as connected.
  * Credentials are written to /etc/smbpal/credentials/ at mode 0600 and are
    never passed on a command line, logged, or written to the Samba
    configuration.
  * Changing what this machine shares, or what it connects to, requires
    authorisation through polkit. Three actions are registered; connecting to
    and disconnecting from an already-configured share is not one of them,
    because an armed automount attaches when any local user lists the
    directory and a password prompt in front of that would protect nothing.
  * Other SMB servers on the network can be browsed, and this machine
    advertises itself only while it is actually sharing something.
  * Removing the package undoes what it did: the include line goes, the units
    are stopped and removed, and smb.conf is left byte-identical to how it was
    found. Purging additionally forgets the configuration in /etc/smbpal.

  * Nobody is added to the "smbpal" group by this package. That membership is
    what permits talking to a service running as root, so it is left to the
    administrator: run "sudo usermod -aG smbpal <user>" and log in again.
    Without it the applications start and cannot reach the daemon.
  * Architecture: all. The same file installs on arm64, armhf and amd64 —
    SMBPal is Python, and depends on the distribution's GTK rather than
    carrying its own.
  * Built and tested against Raspberry Pi OS Trixie on a Raspberry Pi 4.
    Nothing else has been tested.

 -- Luke Hynek <luke.hynek@aiminternet.co.uk>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 21:30:00 +0000
